/*
	RMSTEST 15 — how does a border percentage convert to tiles?

	preview-design Sec.15 item 7c. Sec.4 pins: low-index edges (left, top) take
	floor(pct/100 × dim), high-index edges (right, bottom) take ceil. The only
	evidence is guide:885-886's single worked example — 2% of a 120-wide map
	needs 2 on top/left and 3 on bottom/right. One data point cannot distinguish
	an asymmetric rule from plain rounding that happens to agree at that point,
	and Sec.4 says so.

	FOUR EDGES, FOUR DIFFERENT VALUES, chosen so that every candidate model
	produces a distinct signature. On TINY (120 wide):

	  left   3  -> 3.6   floor 3 | round 4 | ceil 4
	  right  2  -> 2.4   floor 2 | round 2 | ceil 3
	  top    6  -> 7.2   floor 7 | round 7 | ceil 8
	  bottom 9  -> 10.8  floor 10 | round 11 | ceil 11

	READ IT WITH THE PROBE
	  python probe_scenario.py <scenario> --bbox GRASS
	  Read the four "inset" numbers, which are already expressed the same way
	  the border attributes are — distance from each map edge.

	  left=3 right=3 top=7 bottom=11  -> floor on low edges, ceil on high.
	                                     Sec.4 CONFIRMED as written.
	  left=3 right=2 top=7 bottom=10  -> plain floor on all four edges.
	  left=4 right=2 top=7 bottom=11  -> plain round-half-up on all four.
	  left=4 right=3 top=8 bottom=11  -> plain ceil on all four.

	Any other combination: report all four numbers. The values were picked so
	that no two models collide, so an unlisted signature means the rule is
	something none of us proposed.

	THIS ALSO CHECKS TWO OTHER THINGS FOR FREE
	  1. Which attribute maps to which axis. Sec.4 claims left/right are x and
	     top/bottom are y. All four values differ, so if the insets come back
	     transposed the bbox says so immediately.
	  2. border_fuzziness 100. Sec.4 reads the argument as ADHERENCE
	     (guide:903 calls it %BorderAdherence), so 100 should mean a hard stop
	     and a crisp rectangle. If the grass edge comes back ragged instead,
	     that reading is inverted and Sec.6.1's whole border model needs
	     revisiting — which would matter on the 19 corpus maps that set it.

	DESIGN NOTES
	  base_terrain WATER so the grass rectangle's extent is unambiguous.
	  land_percent 100 so the land grows until the borders stop it rather than
	  until a tile budget runs out.
	  Player lands are DESERT, not grass, so they cannot pollute the GRASS
	  bounding box, and circle_radius 5 keeps them near the map centre where
	  they cannot touch any border. They will read as desert holes inside the
	  grass; that is expected and harmless.
	  base_size 1 and land_position 50 50 so the land starts small and central
	  and grows outward into all four borders evenly.

	RUN ON: TINY, 2 players. The arithmetic above is specific to dim 120 —
	on any other size the predicted signatures are different and this header
	is wrong.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain WATER

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type DESERT
	base_size 3
	number_of_tiles 1
	circle_radius 5 0
}

create_land
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	land_position 50 50
	base_size 1
	land_percent 100
	border_fuzziness 100
	left_border 3
	right_border 2
	top_border 6
	bottom_border 9
}
